1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,240
Our exam project has a second part: a small experiment, separate from the quiz app.

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:15,544
The assignment: let a linear regression model and a local language model with RAG predict the same number, then compare them.

3
00:00:16,144 --> 00:00:24,315
First: what linear regression is. Then our task, our code, and the results.

4
00:00:25,915 --> 00:00:32,336
In normal programming, a person writes the rule: input goes in, the rule runs, output comes out.

5
00:00:32,936 --> 00:00:42,024
In machine learning we give the computer examples of inputs with their correct outputs, and it finds the rule itself.

6
00:00:42,624 --> 00:00:46,763
Finding the rule is called training. The finished rule is called a model.

7
00:00:47,363 --> 00:00:53,507
When the output is a number, such as a price, a temperature or a time, the job is called regression.

8
00:00:55,107 --> 00:01:03,107
Put every example on a chart: the input along the bottom, the output up the side. Each example is one dot.

9
00:01:03,707 --> 00:01:07,781
Linear regression draws the straight line that fits the dots best.

10
00:01:08,381 --> 00:01:14,589
A straight line is just two numbers: the slope (how steep) and the intercept (where it starts).

11
00:01:15,189 --> 00:01:23,765
Output = slope × input + intercept. Training means finding those two numbers.

12
00:01:24,365 --> 00:01:28,611
To predict, take a new input, go up to the line, and read off the output.

13
00:01:30,211 --> 00:01:36,013
For each dot, measure how far the line is from it. That gap is the error.

14
00:01:36,613 --> 00:01:42,544
Square every error and add them up. Squaring makes big misses count much more than small ones.

15
00:01:43,144 --> 00:01:50,995
The best line is the one with the smallest total. This method is called least squares.

16
00:01:52,595 --> 00:01:58,227
A model can look good simply because it has already seen the answers.

17
00:01:58,827 --> 00:02:05,077
So we split the data. The training set is used to fit the line. The test set is hidden until the end.

18
00:02:05,677 --> 00:02:13,869
Then we predict the test cases and compare with the true values. The difference is the absolute error.

19
00:02:14,469 --> 00:02:19,632
The average over all test cases is the mean absolute error, MAE. Lower is better.

20
00:02:21,232 --> 00:02:27,376
Our task: predict how long a body takes to orbit the Sun, from its distance to the Sun.

21
00:02:27,976 --> 00:02:35,848
Input: distance in astronomical units (Earth = 1 AU). Output: orbital period in Earth years.

22
00:02:36,448 --> 00:02:44,491
The data comes from the fact tables in the same NASA document the quiz app uses: nine bodies, Mercury to Pluto.

23
00:02:45,091 --> 00:02:52,344
We add a tenth, made-up body at 15 AU. The language model cannot have memorised it.

24
00:02:52,944 --> 00:02:59,621
Seven bodies are for training. Three are for testing: Mars, Uranus and the made-up body.

25
00:03:01,221 --> 00:03:09,136
On a chart our data is not a straight line. It bends upward: far-away bodies take much longer.

26
00:03:09,736 --> 00:03:17,224
A straight line through that curve fits badly. For Mars it predicts minus 2.39 years.

27
00:03:17,824 --> 00:03:26,891
The trick: take the logarithm of both numbers. A logarithm counts the size of a number: 10 becomes 1, 100 becomes 2.

28
00:03:27,491 --> 00:03:33,571
On this log-log chart the same dots fall on a straight line, so linear regression fits.

29
00:03:34,171 --> 00:03:46,480
The slope it finds is 1.4998. Kepler's third law says exactly 1.5: the model rediscovered the law from seven examples.

30
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:54,373
Everything is in one file: experiment/run_experiment.py.

31
00:03:54,973 --> 00:04:05,597
build_dataset() reads distance and orbit period from the PDF with two text patterns, converts the units, and marks each row train or test.

32
00:04:06,197 --> 00:04:11,957
fit_regressions() gets the train rows. x is the distance, y is the period.

33
00:04:12,557 --> 00:04:20,707
One line trains the model: LinearRegression().fit(), from the scikit-learn library, on the logarithms of x and y.

34
00:04:21,307 --> 00:04:27,173
A second model is fitted on the raw numbers, without logarithms, as a baseline to compare with.

35
00:04:27,773 --> 00:04:33,448
predict() gives the logarithm of the period, so 10 to that power turns it back into years.

36
00:04:35,048 --> 00:04:41,939
The other contestant is the local language model with RAG. It gets the same three test distances.

37
00:04:42,539 --> 00:04:50,624
build_store() writes one sentence per training body, with its distance and period, and saves them in a vector database.

38
00:04:51,224 --> 00:04:58,691
The code then checks that no test body and no test answer is in that database, and stops if one is.

39
00:04:59,291 --> 00:05:07,141
For each test case, run_cases() retrieves the 4 most similar sentences and puts them in the prompt as reference examples.

40
00:05:07,741 --> 00:05:14,035
The model must reply with a single number. We ask twice: with the examples, and without.

41
00:05:14,635 --> 00:05:21,461
The language model never sees the regression, its two numbers, or any code. That keeps the comparison fair.

42
00:05:23,061 --> 00:05:35,072
Uranus takes 84.02 years. Log-log regression: 84.00. Straight-line baseline: 107.85.

43
00:05:35,672 --> 00:05:42,904
The language model with RAG says 14.11 years. Without RAG it says 176.35.

44
00:05:43,504 --> 00:05:57,648
Mean absolute error over the three test cases: 0.02 years for log-log regression, 17.24 for the baseline, 38.73 with RAG, 44.83 without.

45
00:05:59,248 --> 00:06:08,144
Regression is built for exactly this: it fits two numbers to a numeric pattern, and here the pattern is a straight line.

46
00:06:08,744 --> 00:06:16,083
The language model predicts likely words. It does not calculate, and it did not work out the pattern from four examples.

47
00:06:16,683 --> 00:06:25,429
Retrieval did not help much: it finds similar text, not nearby numbers. The same four bodies came back for all three questions.

48
00:06:26,029 --> 00:06:35,949
A number that follows a pattern: train a small model. A fact you can quote from a document: use a language model with RAG.

49
00:06:36,549 --> 00:06:45,659
Honest limits: three test cases and one run. And the regression only works because we picked the right shape.
